`isFaSegmentReplay` reads as a pure query, but first sight also **registers** the segment in `tracking.seenFaSegments`:

```javascript
function isFaSegmentReplay(tracking, tokens) {
    if (!tokens.faSessionId || tokens.faSegment == null) return false;
    var segmentKey = tokens.faSessionId + ':' + tokens.faSegment;
    if (tracking.seenFaSegments[segmentKey]) return true;
    tracking.seenFaSegments[segmentKey] = true;   // <-- mutation inside an `is...` query
    return false;
}
```

The doc comment does document it, and behavior is correct (verified: dedupe semantics are byte-identical to the pre-refactor inline block). But the call site — `if (tokens && !isFaSegmentReplay(tracking, tokens)) appendUsageSample(...)` — reads as a stateless check; a future reader reordering or double-calling it would silently change dedupe behavior. A name that carries the side effect (`registerFaSegment`, `markFaSegmentSeen`) or moving the "seen" write next to `appendUsageSample` would make the contract obvious. Pure naming/structure polish — no behavior change requested.
"}

{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `applyLogSignals` takes an implicit `line`/`lower` pair that callers can desynchronize**

```javascript
function applyLogSignals(tracking, line, lower) {
    applyResumeSignal(tracking, parseResumeSignal(line));
    applyRateLimitSignal(tracking, parseRateLimitSignal(line));
    if (isTimeoutLine(lower)) tracking.timeoutCount += 1;
}
```

The contract "`lower` must be `line.toLowerCase()`" is implicit: the timeout check trusts the caller's `lower`, while resume/rate-limit parse `line`. The new characterization test itself demonstrates the looseness — `reporter.applyLogSignals(tracking, 'Feedback loop: resuming agent for polish attempt 1/2', ''.toLowerCase())` passes a mismatched pair and still passes.

Computing `lower` inside `applyLogSignals` (or taking only `line`) makes misuse impossible at identical cost — the loop already lowercases every line exactly once, same as the pre-refactor code. Since `isTimeoutLine(lower)` is now a public-ish export, an internal one-argument wrapper would keep the exported surface honest. Optional polish; current call sites are correct.
"}

